            <ArticleTitle>[An elderly, cachexic patient with a painful leg].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>D942</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 68-year-old female presented with acute abdominal discomfort in the left lower quadrant and a painful left leg. Her medical history only revealed an eating disorder. Physical examination was insignificant. Diagnostic imaging showed an obturator hernia; subsequently, a laparotomy was performed. An obturator hernia, also known as little old ladies' disease, is associated with abdominal pain and a painful leg.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
